sections:[
["Direct answer","Keto and low-carb are related but not identical. Keto usually restricts carbohydrate enough to promote nutritional ketosis, while 'low-carb' covers a much wider range of carbohydrate intakes. For weight management, the important comparison is how each pattern changes total energy intake, food choices, adherence and nutritional adequacy for the person using it."],
["What actually changes","Reducing carbohydrate often removes or shrinks foods such as bread, rice, pasta, sweets and sugar-sweetened drinks. A ketogenic pattern makes the carbohydrate restriction substantially tighter and commonly increases the relative contribution of fat. That change can alter food choice, appetite and early scale weight, but it does not create a separate law of energy balance."],
["What the evidence compares","Randomized trials and systematic reviews can compare low-carbohydrate diets with other dietary patterns over defined periods. A recurring limitation is that studies differ in calorie targets, coaching, adherence and duration. Therefore, a finding from a short controlled trial should not automatically be treated as proof of long-term superiority for every adult."],
["Worked example with FitMe Pro","Take an 80 kg, 175 cm, 30-year-old man. BMI is 80 ÷ 1.75² = 26.1. That BMI number does not tell you whether keto or low-carb will work better; it simply describes weight relative to height. The Calorie Deficit Calculator can then be used to establish an energy-planning starting point while the chosen diet determines which foods supply those calories."],
["Choosing between the patterns","Someone who enjoys eggs, fish, vegetables, dairy, nuts and very low-carbohydrate meals may find keto workable, while another person may prefer a broader low-carb pattern that includes legumes, fruit or whole grains. A useful plan is the one that can meet nutritional needs and be followed without creating unnecessary conflict with culture, budget, training or social life."],
["Important limits","People taking glucose-lowering medication or managing a medical condition should not change carbohydrate intake aggressively without appropriate clinical advice. Neither the word 'keto' nor 'low-carb' tells you whether a diet is nutritionally adequate; the actual foods, portions and overall pattern still have to be assessed."]
],
