sections:[
["Direct answer","Increasing activity can increase energy expenditure without producing the scale change you expected. The gap can arise because exercise expenditure is difficult to estimate, appetite or food intake can change, non-exercise movement can shift, and short-term water changes can obscure the underlying trend. Activity remains useful even when body weight is not falling quickly."],
["The compensation problem","A calorie estimate from a watch or exercise machine is not a direct measurement of how much extra energy the session caused your body to use. After becoming more active, some people may also eat more or move less at other times. These are not signs of failure; they are reasons a simple 'exercise calories minus food calories' calculation can be less precise than it appears."],
["Worked example","An 80 kg, 175 cm adult has BMI 26.1 by the FitMe Pro formula. If that person adds three walking sessions but sees little scale change over a short period, the BMI number itself does not explain the outcome. The relevant comparison is the longer weight trend, food intake, activity consistency and measurement conditions. BMI is a context metric, not a daily progress meter."],
["How to test whether there is a plateau","Use repeated measurements under similar conditions and review several weeks rather than comparing two isolated days. Check whether the new activity was actually completed, whether portions or liquid calories changed, and whether sleep or routine changed. A genuine plateau is a trend problem, not a single number that looks disappointing."],
["What activity is still doing","Regular activity has benefits beyond the scale, including physical fitness and health outcomes. Public-health recommendations are based on population evidence and are not framed as a promise of a particular kilogram loss for every person. This distinction matters: a useful exercise program does not become useless because the scale is slow."],
["When to change the plan","Change one controllable variable at a time when the longer trend justifies it. A large reaction to a short-term fluctuation can create unnecessary restriction or excessive exercise. If weight change is unexplained, unusually rapid, or accompanied by concerning symptoms, individualized clinical advice is more appropriate than repeatedly changing the calculator target."]
],
